  "id": "diagonal-pull",
  "name": "Diagonal Pull",
  "definition": "A strict bilateral seated cable pull whose reviewed start places the extended arms on the source-prescribed 45-degree line and whose concentric path brings a wide straight bar to the chest through primarily sagittal shoulder extension and elbow flexion. Lorenzetti directly measured this exact Technogym Ercolina Rehab fixture with 22-camera three-dimensional motion capture, an inline load cell, and cable markers: the 45-degree pulldown produced the largest sagittal shoulder range of the four compared pulling exercises. The source did not define the 45-degree instruction in an anatomical coordinate system, isolate scapular motion, record EMG, or standardize the observed spinal excursion as absent. The catalog therefore preserves the source-defined start and chest-contact endpoint without publishing a humeral angle, authors no shoulder-adduction or scapular prime action, treats the scapula and trunk as stability demands, and labels the muscle hierarchy as mechanics-derived rather than directly measured. Vertical overhead pulldowns, horizontal rows, flared rear-delt rows, straight-arm pulldowns, commercial lever high rows, rotational diagonal patterns, and any version using deliberate trunk or lower-body propulsion remain outside this contract.",
